this video isn't about the science of  burnout  or how to identify if you have it  because you know  i'm not a licensed therapist or doctor  but what i do want to talk about  is a theory for why i think burnout is  so  common especially in the tech industry  on the anonymous professional networking  platform blind  61 percent of professionals in february  self-reporting themselves  as being burnt out and that number has  only risen to 73 percent  because of kovid three months after the  initial poll  some tech companies are notorious for  burning out their employees  and the platform has numerous threads of  folks sharing their  own burnout stories the unmanageable  workload  the expectation to constantly perform  perfectly  or the evaluation of performance based  on output which then creates this  toxic productivity focused work  environment these things  are not entirely uncommon in the tech  industry i've experienced burnout in  basically  every job that i've had so i was trying  to accept that it's just  a part of tech life but after i stepped  away from working in a corporate  environment  i thought does it have to be but first  let's define burnout because it can mean  a lot of different things  so basically in a nutshell that's how i  want to frame this video  it happens when the reward we get is not  worth the effort that we put in  this equation the balance between reward  and effort  is really what i want to focus on today  so let's talk about the effort side of  the equation  first i just want to say with like every  year that passes by  and the more that i learn about coding  in tech  i've concluded it takes a hell of a lot  of effort just to make something  not only does it take individuals to  learn and master technologies that are  current and state of the art  but they then need to use that knowledge  and spend hours and hours  building something that's probably never  existed before  in the same way that alone is a lot of  work  while on top of that all the  coordination and communication that  needs to happen before  any building takes place to make sure  that they're building the right thing to  begin with  which then is super subjective because  it depends on the product managers  designers  executives and anybody else with power  on  their goals and purpose for their work  then once it's  built making sure everything works okay  figuring out how to deliver the thing  telling users about the thing and then  finally  making sure it keeps working once it's  delivered  honestly sometimes i think it's a  miracle that things get built and that  we have the tech that we do  because in each step a lot of things can  go wrong  like realizing too late that you're  solving the wrong problem  or that something isn't technically  feasible or that two of the leaders of  your project  don't see eye to eye on how to solve a  problem because  one of them wants a promotion and the  other one wants to solve a problem for a  very specific user  so yeah creating technology takes a lot  of effort and a lot of things can go  wrong in the process that could prevent  something from being built at all  so already one side of the reward versus  effort equation  is pretty heavy so naturally i think a  lot of people would expect a lot of  reward  for that amount of work so now let's  talk about that side of the equation  what each of us see as rewarding is  completely subjective  and a lot of it is based on our  backgrounds personality  interests and values some people value  financial stability  others value the flexibility to do what  they want to in life  some value status and external  validation  and others are in pursuit of feeling  like they're making a difference in the  world  and the best thing is for each  individual to evaluate  what tangible goals these values  translate to  based on who they are and their own  situation  but what i've noticed is that in the  tech industry we tend to hype up the  same tangible goals  so things like getting a job at a fan  company making a big salary  getting a promotion or getting a job at  a mission-driven company  or what we've been able to think of so  far is how we know we've  made it underlying all of this obviously  are the values that are core to each  individual person  but for some reason we've accepted that  those goals are the ones that everybody  should strive for regardless of their  situation  it can be helpful to have quorum on how  our values manifest in the real world  like it can help spur inspiration or  help point towards a direction where we  might want to explore  it's the benefit of walking down a path  that's been walked down before  because we can use others journeys as  checkpoints for our own  even while so many people have their own  unique story  into the industry at the end of the day  it feels like  everyone ends up at a fang company at  some point  even if that's not true it sure feels  like it because that's how we framed  success  but i think when we internalize these  few paths our  imagination becomes limited and we start  believing that these paths  are like the only ones out there we  might forget what we  actually want for ourselves when we  start to see  other people's paths as our own and when  we achieve those goals that  we you know maybe didn't even really  want in the first place we feel  disappointed because the reward doesn't  come  and it just wasn't worth it my theory is  that this is precisely one of the many  causes of burnout in tech  if burnout is a failure to balance  reward and effort  we put a lot of effort in for work that  we end up realizing  are not really that rewarding for  ourselves  it can be difficult to manage this too  since you don't know if something's  going to be worth it or not until you  put in that effort first  i think this has a lot to do with why  the average tenure for a software  engineer  is two years as in after giving two  years of solid effort most people  evaluate whether this job is worth it or  not  i've seen this in my own career so far  one of the values that i use as my north  star  is to help people with the work that i  do so when i started to work at a  mission-driven company with a mission  i really aligned with i was ecstatic so  i poured my heart and soul into working  on the ios  app for them commuting three hours a day  for that job  but i realized two years later that the  leaders of this company didn't really  see the app that i worked on as a key  part of their strategy  and therefore my work wasn't actually  really helping anyone despite the work i  put in  burnout or when i worked at a fang  company because i was under the  impression that my work would reach  millions of people and make a big impact  so i poured my heart and soul into  working on features  collaborating with people from other  teams and then seeing that at the end  the work that i did didn't really feel  like it did anything  especially because it was hard to see  how it contributed to the product  outside of a few graphs that showed me a  few metrics moved by  tiny fractions of a percent burnout  and like i'm so grateful to have even  had these opportunities to begin with  because  i know not everyone does and it wasn't  like there was any one person  who was responsible for the outcome  either i kind of just  accepted that maybe this is just how it  is in tech  software engineering takes a lot of  effort i think that's true no matter  where you look in the industry  so the effort side of the burnout  equation is fixed  so what can we do about the other side  of the equation the reward  well for one the tech community is  always gonna hype up things it's just  the laws of popular culture but i think  we as individuals can view this from a  different lens to  help us better understand the industry  and ourselves  when you look closely at these things  that we're all supposedly striving for  there are fundamental rewards behind  them i think when people strive for the  common goals what they're really after  are  those rewards but burnout happens  because when they achieve them they  might not get the reward they were  expecting for the effort they put in  probably because they were pursuing a  goal they didn't really want in the  first place  so i guess my theory for one way to  alleviate burnout in tech  is for us as an industry to be more  imaginative  and challenge ourselves to find our  rewards through ways that feel authentic  to us heck there are plenty of people  who are doing this already  those who've never worked at fang those  who don't work in silicon valley maybe  have a traditional nine-to-five  and have found ways to navigate their  career in a way that feels authentic to  them how might we learn from them  and i think it's also important for each  of us to reflect on our current  situation and ask  is the reward i'm getting worth the  effort that i'm putting in  is it what i expected or wanted all  along managing that fine balance between  reward and effort is really tricky of  course the common ways that folks often  talk about curing burnout are a part of  this too  so taking vacations and taking breaks  and managing healthy relationships with  your coworkers  but i think the real first step is to do  that evaluation  am i getting the reward i expect for the  effort that i put in  it might be uncomfortable to sit with  that reality but i think  it's important to be honest with  ourselves and sit with that discomfort  rather than like  prioritize it away out of fear that  we're just simply not getting what we  put into our jobs  because when we say with it and we see  it we're able to start figuring out  what's going on in the first place and  that's always the first step once we can  do that  we can start imagining how we might make  changes big or small  to get the reward each of us need for  the effort we put in  sure it might mean pursuing something  that no one's done before  and that definitely comes with risk and  therefore is a privilege to actually go  after it but i know that we can't go on  like this until literally  everyone in the industry is burned out  because how are we going to use  technology to help society  if we're not helping ourselves speaking  of exploring what's out there  and being imaginative with what other  things you can do with technology  this video is sponsored by trends the  ultimate knowledge hub from the hustle  it's a community built for entrepreneurs  founders  investors and ambitious people to gather  learn  workshop and collaborate trends gives  you the information and network you need  to be successful  and it's a great place to jump start  your ideas and workshop with  like-minded builders i found it as a  great place to find  what's going on in different industries  like how the paper notebook business has  done  and also what trends you can trade on in  japan and  there's a ton of opportunities to  network with entrepreneurs and people  who are really thinking about what's  next in the industry  so right now you can get your first two  weeks for just one dollar  go to trends dot co slash my yuko  for your one dollar two week trial thank  you so much to transfer sponsoring  today's video  if you like this video then make sure to  subscribe and let me know what your  thoughts are about burnout in the  comments down below  i'll see you next time and take care  everybody bye
 

